values
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:69703d40e869','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is one of the fundamental principles of British life?',array['The rule of law','Membership of a trade union','Rule by a single party','Loyalty to one religion']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The fundamental principles of British life include democracy, the rule of law, individual liberty, tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs, and participation in community life.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:0e654e95e857','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is NOT one of the fundamental principles of British life?',array['Participation in community life','Individual liberty','Democracy','Compulsory voting']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The fundamental principles are democracy, the rule of law, individual liberty, tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs, and participation in community life. Voting is not compulsory.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:067daa8dab13','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO are fundamental principles of British life?',array['Democracy and individual liberty','Individual liberty and a state newspaper','Democracy and military service','Military service and a state newspaper']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Democracy and individual liberty are two of the fundamental principles of British life.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:bc572ac27b71','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs is one of the fundamental principles of British life.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs is a fundamental principle of British life.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:58909df98937','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: British values and principles are based only on written laws, not on history or traditions.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The fundamental principles of British life are based on history and traditions and are protected by law, customs and expectations.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:9485169698ef','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, the fundamental principles of British life are based on:',array['the wishes of the monarch alone','a single religious text','history and traditions','rules made by local councils']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): British values are based on history and traditions and are protected by law, customs and expectations.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:b7cbeabe37c3','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','If you wish to be a permanent resident or citizen of the UK, which of these should you do?',array['Attend a place of worship','Join a political party','Respect and obey the law','Buy a house in the UK']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Anyone who wishes to be a permanent resident or citizen should respect and obey the law.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:df63707d5791','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: People who want to live permanently in the UK should look after the area in which they live and the environment.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Looking after the area in which you live and the environment is one of the responsibilities listed for permanent residents and citizens.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:a98adb63819b','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Respecting the rights of others includes respecting their right to their own opinions.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Permanent residents and citizens should respect the rights of others, including their right to their own opinions.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:172b32be0164','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is a freedom that the UK offers to everyone living here?',array['A guaranteed job','Free housing for everyone','Freedom from paying tax','Freedom of speech']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): In return for the responsibilities, the UK offers freedoms including freedom of belief and religion and freedom of speech.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:0ea3155d8e8d','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which right does the UK offer to everyone living here?',array['The right to a fair trial','The right to a company car','The right to choose which laws to obey','The right to free private healthcare']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The freedoms the UK offers include the right to a fair trial.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:ab78a1e403bd','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO are freedoms that the UK offers to people living here?',array['Freedom of speech and free housing','The right to a fair trial and a guaranteed job','Freedom of speech and the right to a fair trial','Free housing and a guaranteed job']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The UK offers freedoms including freedom of speech, freedom of belief and religion, freedom from unfair discrimination, the right to a fair trial and the right to join in the election of a government.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:3874f7878b96','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is a responsibility of people who want to live permanently in the UK?',array['Owning their own home','Treating others with fairness','Speaking more than one language','Belonging to a sports club']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Permanent residents and citizens should treat others with fairness.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:1734761eb274','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Among the responsibilities listed in the handbook, people should look after:',array['only people of their own faith','the monarch''s palaces','themselves and their family','their employer''s property']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): People who wish to be permanent residents or citizens should look after themselves and their family.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:cd877d20936f','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The right to join in the election of a government is one of the freedoms people in the UK enjoy.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The freedoms offered by the UK include the right to join in the election of a government.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:c919728a5460','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','At a citizenship ceremony, new citizens make an oath or affirmation of allegiance and a:',array['tax return','job application','speech','pledge']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Applicants take part in a citizenship ceremony, where they make an oath (or affirmation) and a pledge.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:19b6f5961cc7','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the oath or affirmation of allegiance, new citizens promise to be faithful to:',array['the Archbishop of Canterbury','the monarch, their heirs and successors','their local Member of Parliament','the Prime Minister of the day']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): In the oath or affirmation, new citizens promise to be faithful and bear true allegiance to the monarch, their heirs and successors, according to law.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:c1d79a1d9884','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In the citizenship pledge, new citizens promise to uphold the UK''s democratic values.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The pledge includes: ''I will uphold its democratic values.''',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:28392a58eb4d','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these promises is part of the citizenship pledge?',array['To observe the UK''s laws faithfully','To serve in the armed forces','To live in the UK for the rest of their life','To vote in every general election']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The pledge includes: ''I will observe its laws faithfully and fulfil my duties and obligations as a British citizen.''',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:efade9242dac','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: A person who does not wish to swear an oath by God can make an affirmation instead.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): At the citizenship ceremony applicants make either an oath (sworn by God) or an affirmation, which has no religious wording.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:96ff62fb20fc','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','To become a permanent resident or a British citizen, you normally need to:',array['join a UK political party and vote in an election','speak and read English and have a good understanding of life in the UK','have been born in a Commonwealth country','own a property and have a UK driving licence']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): To apply to become a permanent resident or citizen you need to be able to speak and read English and have a good understanding of life in the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:e2d31970e59e','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The questions in the Life in the UK test are based on the official handbook.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The test questions are based on all parts of the handbook ''Life in the United Kingdom: A Guide for New Residents''.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:cd449e4a91e3','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where can you take the Life in the UK test?',array['At any public library','At a registered and approved test centre','Online from home','At your local post office']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): You can only take the test at a registered and approved Life in the UK test centre.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:cc10346391b6','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Life in the UK test is usually taken by people who are applying for:',array['a UK driving licence','settlement or British citizenship','a National Insurance number','a student loan']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Passing the Life in the UK test is part of the requirements for people applying for settlement (permanent residence) or British citizenship.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:ac0a624ffb46','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Only British citizens, and not other people living in the UK, are expected to respect and obey the law.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The responsibilities and freedoms that flow from the fundamental principles are shared by all those living in the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:8c2e2d3f6e07','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is NOT listed in the handbook as a responsibility of people who want to live permanently in the UK?',array['Treating others with fairness','Attending a religious service every week','Looking after the area where they live','Respecting and obeying the law']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The listed responsibilities include respecting and obeying the law, respecting the rights of others, treating others with fairness, looking after yourself and your family, and looking after your area and the environment. Religion is a free choice.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:b5762a30c50e','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Individual liberty means people in the UK may break the law if they disagree with it.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The rule of law is a fundamental principle of British life: everyone must respect and obey the law, whatever their personal views.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:8003710fb883','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which fundamental principle of British life is about the adult population having a say in how the country is governed?',array['Individual liberty','Democracy','The rule of law','Tolerance']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Democracy is one of the fundamental principles of British life: the adult population chooses its government.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:fe84ff25ea05','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO are responsibilities of people who want to live permanently in the UK?',array['Owning a home and treating others fairly','Obeying the law and voting in every election','Obeying the law and treating others fairly','Owning a home and voting in every election']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Permanent residents and citizens should respect and obey the law and treat others with fairness.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:058addc0a1db','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which countries make up the United Kingdom?',array['England, Scotland, Wales and the Republic of Ireland','England, Wales, Northern Ireland and the Isle of Man','England, Scotland and Wales only','England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The UK is made up of England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:804c58dd8aee','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which countries make up Great Britain?',array['England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland','England, Scotland and Wales','England, Scotland and Northern Ireland','England and Wales only']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): Great Britain refers only to England, Scotland and Wales.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:4e2e21e69f56','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the official name of the country?',array['The United Kingdom of England and Ireland','The British Union of England and Scotland','The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland','The Kingdom of Great Britain and the Isles']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The official name of the country is the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:cc08d0935c14','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Channel Islands and the Isle of Man are part of the United Kingdom.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The Channel Islands and the Isle of Man are not part of the UK; they are Crown dependencies with their own governments.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:fc2e22021023','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What are the Channel Islands and the Isle of Man known as?',array['Royal counties','British overseas territories','Devolved nations','Crown dependencies']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The Channel Islands and the Isle of Man are Crown dependencies, closely linked with the UK but not part of it.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:d95586f8803d','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO are British overseas territories?',array['Jersey and the Falkland Islands','Jersey and the Isle of Man','St Helena and the Isle of Man','St Helena and the Falkland Islands']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): There are several British overseas territories linked to the UK, such as St Helena and the Falkland Islands. Jersey and the Isle of Man are Crown dependencies.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:7696c75965ed','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Crown dependencies have their own governments.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The Channel Islands and the Isle of Man have their own governments and are called Crown dependencies.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:9739e96cfc6c','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which cross on the Union Flag represents Scotland?',array['A red diagonal cross on a white ground','A yellow cross on a black ground','A white diagonal cross on a blue ground','A red cross on a white ground']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The cross of St Andrew, patron saint of Scotland, is a white diagonal cross on a blue ground.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:402522fec30c','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which patron saint''s cross on the Union Flag represents Ireland?',array['St Patrick','St David','St Columba','St Andrew']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The cross of St Patrick, patron saint of Ireland, is a red diagonal cross on a white ground.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:931e2379d78c','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Wales is represented on the Union Flag.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The Union Flag combines the crosses of St George, St Andrew and St Patrick. Wales is not represented on it.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:28479e17be34','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why is the Welsh dragon not on the Union Flag?',array['Wales joined the United Kingdom after 1801','The dragon was thought too difficult to show','Wales chose not to be included in the design','Wales was already united with England when the first Union Flag was created']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The Welsh dragon does not appear because, when the first Union Flag was created, Wales was already united with England.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:5b50b40d383b','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the national flag of the UK often called?',array['The St George','The Royal Standard','The Union Jack','The Red Ensign']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The flag of the UK is the Union Flag, often called the Union Jack.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:8618e1d7aa29','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO cities are capitals of countries in the UK?',array['Cardiff and Glasgow','Manchester and Edinburgh','Glasgow and Manchester','Cardiff and Edinburgh']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The capital cities are London (England), Edinburgh (Scotland), Cardiff (Wales) and Belfast (Northern Ireland).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:d27057cbfa3d','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the capital city of Northern Ireland?',array['Londonderry','Dublin','Armagh','Belfast']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): Belfast is the capital city of Northern Ireland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:b493cab666ec','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Glasgow is the capital city of Scotland.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): Edinburgh is the capital city of Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:ebdc16db3051','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the currency of the UK?',array['The guinea','The crown','The euro','The pound sterling']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The currency in the UK is the pound sterling; there are 100 pence in a pound.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:bfb552f3c5d1','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Banknotes issued in Scotland and Northern Ireland are valid everywhere in the UK.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): Northern Ireland and Scotland have their own banknotes, which are valid everywhere in the UK, although shops and businesses do not have to accept them.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:669c1481f27f','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where is Gaelic, a different language from English, spoken?',array['In some parts of the Highlands and Islands of Scotland','In the Channel Islands','In Cardiff and the Welsh valleys','In the south-west of England']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): In Scotland, Gaelic is spoken in some parts of the Highlands and Islands.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:72d6922e6662','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In Northern Ireland, a number of people speak Irish Gaelic.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): A number of people in Northern Ireland speak Irish Gaelic.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:0207d4506ee4','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO languages, other than English, are spoken in parts of the UK?',array['Flemish and Breton','Flemish and Gaelic','Welsh and Breton','Welsh and Gaelic']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): Many people in Wales speak Welsh, which is taught in schools and universities, and Gaelic is spoken in parts of Scotland (Irish Gaelic in Northern Ireland).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:1f1625c24eba','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When is St David''s Day?',array['23 April','1 March','17 March','30 November']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): St David is the patron saint of Wales; his day is 1 March.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:a7a94ef87c27','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who is the patron saint of Scotland?',array['St Andrew','St David','St Patrick','St George']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): St Andrew is the patron saint of Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:a2d9c67eef21','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','On which date is St George''s Day, the day of England''s patron saint?',array['17 March','1 March','23 April','30 November']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): St George''s Day, England, is 23 April.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:82876c111740','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','St Patrick''s Day, 17 March, is the patron saint''s day of which part of the UK?',array['Wales','Scotland','England','Northern Ireland']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): St Patrick is the patron saint of Northern Ireland; his day is 17 March.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:a4b1a65281f5','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO parts of the UK have their patron saint''s day as an official holiday?',array['England and Wales','Wales and Northern Ireland','England and Scotland','Scotland and Northern Ireland']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Only Scotland and Northern Ireland have their patron saint''s day as an official holiday (although in Scotland not all businesses close).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:55a9963f031d','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Christmas Day, 25 December, celebrates:',array['the rising of Jesus from the dead','the death of Jesus Christ','the birth of Jesus Christ','the start of Lent']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Christmas Day celebrates the birth of Jesus Christ and is a public holiday.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:35b61ac78224','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When is Boxing Day?',array['26 December','31 December','2 January','24 December']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Boxing Day is the day after Christmas Day, 26 December, and is a public holiday.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:3d95b9f6c95e','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is Shrove Tuesday also known as?',array['Good Friday','Ash Wednesday','Pancake Day','Mothering Sunday']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Shrove Tuesday, the day before Lent starts, is known as Pancake Day.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:31a83d8aea6e','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How long is Lent, the period before Easter?',array['30 days','40 days','7 days','12 days']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Lent is the 40 days before Easter.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:29ec8270a529','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Easter marks the death of Jesus Christ on Good Friday and his rising from the dead on Easter Sunday.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Easter takes place in March or April and marks the death of Jesus on Good Friday and his rising from the dead on Easter Sunday.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:658a56a7e387','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is Hogmanay?',array['New Year''s Eve in Scotland','St Andrew''s Day in Scotland','A Welsh spring festival','A Scottish harvest festival']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): In Scotland, 31 December is called Hogmanay; 2 January is also a public holiday there.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:8e7984518af1','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Diwali is celebrated by:',array['Muslims and Jews','Christians and Jews','Buddhists only','Hindus and Sikhs']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Diwali is celebrated by Hindus and Sikhs; it is often called the Festival of Lights.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:43ee657b0489','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which city is famous for its Diwali celebrations?',array['Aberdeen','Belfast','Leicester','Swansea']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): There is a famous Diwali celebration held in Leicester.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:d05e7ddc66e6','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which festival lasts eight days and remembers the Jewish people''s struggle for religious freedom?',array['Hannukah','Eid al-Fitr','Diwali','Vaisakhi']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Hannukah is in November or December and is celebrated for eight days; a candle is lit on a stand (a menorah) each day.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:98f283771062','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Eid al-Fitr celebrates:',array['the end of Ramadan','the victory of good over evil','the start of Lent','the founding of the Khalsa']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Eid al-Fitr celebrates the end of Ramadan, when Muslims have fasted for a month.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:cadcbf4e1279','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Eid ul Adha remembers that the prophet Ibrahim was willing to sacrifice his son when God ordered him to.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Eid ul Adha remembers that the prophet Ibrahim was willing to sacrifice his son when God ordered him to.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:6c1613fb5885','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Vaisakhi is a Sikh festival that celebrates:',array['the founding of the Sikh community known as the Khalsa','the end of the month of Ramadan','the beginning of the new year in Scotland','the victory of good over evil']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Vaisakhi (also spelled Baisakhi) is a Sikh festival celebrating the founding of the Sikh community known as the Khalsa.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:a2e818b3e9fe','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','On which day do people play jokes on each other until midday?',array['1 April','5 November','31 October','14 February']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): April Fool''s Day, 1 April, is a day when people play jokes on each other until midday.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:994ebf0f7eb4','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When is Mothering Sunday?',array['The third Sunday in June','The Sunday three weeks before Easter','The second Sunday in May','The Sunday after Easter']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Mothering Sunday (Mother''s Day) is the Sunday three weeks before Easter.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:8f50c26a4d75','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Bonfire Night, 5 November, remembers:',array['the coronation of King James I in 1603','the end of the First World War in 1918','a failed plan in 1605 to blow up the Houses of Parliament','the defeat of the Spanish Armada in 1588']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): In 1605 a group of Catholics led by Guy Fawkes failed in their plan to kill the Protestant king with a bomb in the Houses of Parliament.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:fb31c40da520','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Halloween, on 31 October, has its roots in an ancient pagan festival that marked:',array['the start of spring','the first day of Lent','the longest day of summer','the beginning of winter']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Halloween is an ancient festival with roots in the pagan festival to mark the beginning of winter.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:7b91f32b0996','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Remembrance Day, 11 November, commemorates:',array['the birthday of the monarch','those who died fighting for the UK and its allies','the opening of the first Parliament','the signing of Magna Carta']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Remembrance Day commemorates those who died fighting for the UK and its allies. It originally commemorated the dead of the First World War, which ended on 11 November 1918.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:f51f33e8cb4d','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What do many people wear in the period before Remembrance Day?',array['White paper roses','Green ribbons','Yellow daffodils','Red paper poppies']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): People wear poppies (the red flower found on the battlefields of the First World War).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-04:a170c44c7285','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: On Remembrance Day there is a two-minute silence at 12 noon.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): At 11.00 am on 11 November there is a two-minute silence.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-04',null,null,null,null),
